WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:22.480
The sensor is the primary element of a measurement chain that converts the input variable into

00:22.480 --> 00:25.980
a signal that is suitable for measurement.

00:25.980 --> 00:32.680
A transducer is a device that accepts information in the form of a physical or chemical input variable

00:32.680 --> 00:39.680
and converts it into an output variable that can be of the same or of a different nature.

00:39.680 --> 00:44.960
In other words, we can say that the transducer outputs an electrical signal that is linked

00:44.960 --> 00:51.940
to the input, that is, there is always a relationship between the input and the output.

00:51.940 --> 00:58.920
Here in the slide you can see a diagram that includes the sensor and after it the transducer.

00:58.920 --> 01:03.180
The element we are going to measure is called the measurement.

01:03.180 --> 01:09.260
In the diagram you can see the first measurement that is the input of the sensor and the second

01:09.260 --> 01:13.940
measurement that is the input of the transducer.

01:13.940 --> 01:23.180
So we said that the sensor is the first element in the measurement chain.

01:23.180 --> 01:29.660
The sensor converts the physical quantity into another that is more easily manageable.

01:29.660 --> 01:34.920
The transducer instead provides a measurable electrical signal as output.

01:34.920 --> 01:41.120
In fact, the main difference between the sensor and the transducer is due to the aim of their

01:41.120 --> 01:47.920
using, because the sensor is used for controlling, while the transducer is aimed at the measurement.

01:47.920 --> 01:51.920
That's why we are used to use these two terms as synonyms.

01:51.920 --> 02:00.080
It's important to remember that a transducer is a sensor, but a sensor is not necessarily

02:00.080 --> 02:03.080
a transducer.

02:03.080 --> 02:12.920
There are different types of sensors depending on certain parameters by which they are classified.

02:12.920 --> 02:20.960
In fact, we can have active sensors, passive sensors, digital, analog sensors, and so on.

02:20.960 --> 02:26.480
So we say that sensors can be classified, first of all, according to the type of measurement

02:26.480 --> 02:30.920
.

02:30.920 --> 02:37.080
According to the type of output, voltage, current, resistive, capacitive, inductive sensor, we

02:37.080 --> 02:44.080
have voltage and current sensors, analog sensors that provide the output signal that follows

02:44.080 --> 02:56.080
the variation of the input value, and digital sensors for which the output is a binary code.

02:56.080 --> 03:02.080
Now we want to put attention to the first classification, active and passive.

03:02.080 --> 03:09.080
A sensor is defined active if the physical effect on which it is based ensures the transformation

03:09.080 --> 03:14.080
of the measurement's own energy into an electrical energy.

03:14.080 --> 03:21.080
A sensor is defined passive if the effect of the measurement results in an impedance change

03:21.080 --> 03:23.080
of the sensing element.

03:23.080 --> 03:30.080
In other simpler words, we can say that active sensors generate a current or voltage signal

03:30.080 --> 03:35.080
signal at the output, while for passive sensors we will have a variation at the output of

03:35.080 --> 03:41.080
an electrical parameter, resistance, capacity, and so on.

03:41.080 --> 03:53.080
It is important to underline that for passive sensors we need another conversion.

03:53.080 --> 04:00.080
This conversion is possible using a conditioning circuit, that is defined as a circuit or electrical

04:00.080 --> 04:05.080
system that is able to modify signal characteristics.

04:05.080 --> 04:12.080
It is necessary to clarify that for passive sensors or transducers the conditioning circuit is essential,

04:12.080 --> 04:21.080
is indispensable, while for active sensors or transducers the conditioning circuit is only to make an adaptation

04:21.080 --> 04:23.080
of the parameters.

04:23.080 --> 04:30.080
The conditioning circuit is the task of adapting the parameters of the electrical energy generated by the transducers

04:30.080 --> 04:38.080
transducers to the input characteristics of the measurement system, that is called signal conditioning.

04:38.080 --> 04:45.080
So let's try to understand what happens in case of passive sensors or transducers.

04:45.080 --> 04:49.080
So why this conditioning circuit is so essential?

04:49.080 --> 04:55.080
In general we can say that a single electrical parameter or a current signal has to be further adapted

04:55.080 --> 05:00.080
and then provided as input to our electrical circuit.

05:00.080 --> 05:06.080
Specifically, what are the possible functions of a conditioning circuit?

05:06.080 --> 05:09.080
First of all, application.

05:09.080 --> 05:11.080
Secondary, the filtering.

05:11.080 --> 05:17.080
Perhaps because of the presence of noise or distortion on the user for signal input to the AD converter.

05:17.080 --> 05:23.080
And in this case is the use of a filter is essential.

05:23.080 --> 05:28.080
It might be a resistance voltage or current voltage conversion.

05:28.080 --> 05:32.080
And finally, linearization.

05:32.080 --> 05:37.080
This is an operation that is performed on the analog outputs of non-linear transducers.

05:43.080 --> 05:46.080
Let's continue with more definitions.

05:46.080 --> 05:50.080
Let's then talk about the main features of a sensor.

05:50.080 --> 05:51.080
They are

05:51.080 --> 05:54.080
measure that is the quantity to be measured.

05:54.080 --> 05:59.080
Measuring principle, that is the physical principle on which the conversion is based.

05:59.080 --> 06:06.080
Significant properties, sensor construction properties, internal circuits, sensitivity, and so on.

06:06.080 --> 06:11.080
And range that defines the operating limits of the sensor.

06:11.080 --> 06:21.080
Let's take a detailed look at the characteristics of the inputs.

06:21.080 --> 06:24.080
Species, that is the physical input quantity.

06:24.080 --> 06:32.080
Input range, range of values of the measurement within which the sensor operates according to specifications.

06:32.080 --> 06:41.080
Measureant safety range, that is the range of values of the measurement outside which the sensor remains permanently damaged.

06:41.080 --> 06:47.080
Its extreme values are called overload or overrange.

06:47.080 --> 06:57.080
Similarly also for outputs, we can say that the main characteristics are

06:57.080 --> 07:00.080
Spaces, nature of the output quantity.

07:00.080 --> 07:08.080
Normal operating range, or output range, that is the range of values of the output, when the input varies in the input range.

07:08.080 --> 07:17.080
Deliberable power, the limit value of the power that the sensor can supply to the downstream user system.

07:17.080 --> 07:22.080
In other words, it is the maximum value of the output power.

07:22.080 --> 07:27.080
Moreover, if the output is current, the load impedance is specified.

07:27.080 --> 07:32.080
Output impedance, each sensor has its own impedance.

07:32.080 --> 07:41.080
And uncertainty of output, that is the width of the range, including all the values that could be assumed,

07:41.080 --> 07:50.080
with a certain probability or confidence level, to represent the value of the output corresponding to a certain operating condition.

07:50.080 --> 08:04.080
Well, now let's give an overview of what are the static and dynamic characteristics of sensors and transducers.

08:04.080 --> 08:08.080
Let's start with the static characteristics of a sensor.

08:08.080 --> 08:17.080
First of all, conversion function, that is a function that allows the value of the output to be derived from the input.

08:17.080 --> 08:29.080
Calibration function, that is the relationship that allows the value of the input and the corresponding uncertainty band to be derived from each value of the output quantity.

08:29.080 --> 08:36.080
We are therefore talking about the relationship between input and output, but with a delta of uncertainty.

08:36.080 --> 08:44.080
This is because for each input, we will not have a single output value, but a range of values of the measurement.

08:44.080 --> 08:51.080
Calibration constant, slope of the calibration curve, if it is linear.

08:51.080 --> 08:56.080
And calibration uncertainty, that is the width of the range of values.

08:56.080 --> 09:14.080
Other static characteristics are sensitivity, that is the slope of the conversion curve at a certain point and corresponds to the inverse of the slope of the calibration curve.

09:14.080 --> 09:27.080
In other words, it is the ratio of the change in output signal to the corresponding change in input signal as shown in the formula on the right side of the slide.

09:27.080 --> 09:36.080
Stability, that is the ability to maintain the operating characteristics unchanged over time, long and short term.

09:36.080 --> 09:52.080
And then we cannot forget the linearity, which indicates by how much the calibration curve deviates from the straight line, precisely from the ideal line.

09:52.080 --> 09:59.080
Here we have other three characteristics.

09:59.080 --> 10:02.080
We are still talking about static features.

10:02.080 --> 10:11.080
Resolution, changing the value of the measurement that causes an appreciable change in the value of the output quantity.

10:11.080 --> 10:16.080
If the sensor works close to zero, it is called a threshold.

10:16.080 --> 10:25.080
Repeatability, that is the aptitude of the instrument to provide values of the output quantity that differ little from each other,

10:25.080 --> 10:30.080
when you apply the same input and under the same operating conditions.

10:30.080 --> 10:34.080
It is expressed similarly to calibration uncertainty.

10:34.080 --> 10:42.080
In other words, it is the aptitude of the instrument to always provide the same value at the output for the same input.

10:42.080 --> 10:51.080
In stereesis, this is the maximum difference between the values of the output corresponding to the same measurement,

10:51.080 --> 11:01.080
when all values of the measuring range are considered, and each value is reached with the first increasing and then decreasing measurement.

11:01.080 --> 11:08.080
In other words, it represents the maximum difference between the input and the output values,

11:08.080 --> 11:16.080
and it is calculated by varying the values between the range from minimum to maximum and opposite.

11:16.080 --> 11:26.080
Here, there are dynamic characteristics.

11:26.080 --> 11:35.080
Clearly, we are going to consider separately what concerns the time domain and the frequency domain.

11:35.080 --> 11:40.080
About the frequency domain, we can talk about frequency response,

11:40.080 --> 11:53.080
which we know characterizes the behavior of the sensor, instrument or acquisition system when the input quantity is subjected to time varying stresses.

11:53.080 --> 12:03.080
The frequency range, that is the range of frequencies in which the modulus response curve does not go outside a predetermined tolerance band,

12:03.080 --> 12:08.080
and possible resonance frequency.

12:08.080 --> 12:18.080
About the time domain, we can talk about rise time and response time, time constant, settling time, overshoot dumping factor,

12:18.080 --> 12:21.080
and things I'm sure you've heard before at the university.

12:29.080 --> 12:37.080
From here, in the next two slides, we can find a table of sensors sorted by type.

12:38.080 --> 12:54.080
Now, let's start with the actuators.

12:54.080 --> 13:01.080
By definition, an actuator is a system that includes both mechanical and electrical energy.

13:01.080 --> 13:08.080
In particular, the electrical energy is able to cause the development of a specific motion.

13:08.080 --> 13:16.080
Just as we have seen for sensors, also for the actuators, there are different types.

13:16.080 --> 13:23.080
Electric, electrostatic, hydraulic, electromechanic, and so on, which we will see in the following slides.

13:23.080 --> 13:39.080
So, we can say that an actuator is a device in an automation system that transforms an automatic common decision,

13:39.080 --> 13:46.080
process it by an electronic control board, into a physical action on the processing being regulated.

13:46.080 --> 13:56.080
So, an actuator makes it possible to act on a system in order to change its behavior and obtain the desired output.

13:56.080 --> 14:06.080
More generally, the actuator can be defined as a transducer that is capable of transforming the signal from one physical domain,

14:06.080 --> 14:13.080
typically electrical, into an equivalent quantity belonging to another domain,

14:13.080 --> 14:24.080
for example, mechanical, thermal, hydraulic, and so on.

14:24.080 --> 14:28.080
We can classify the actuators according different parameters.

14:28.080 --> 14:35.080
First of all, we have a classification according to the type of physical quantity that is going to be implemented,

14:35.080 --> 14:39.080
mechanical, hydraulic, electric, and so on.

14:39.080 --> 14:45.080
And the second one, according to the type of electric actuation common,

14:45.080 --> 14:50.080
that could be digital, analog, or PWM.

14:50.080 --> 14:58.080
Let's talk about an example of an actuator.

14:58.080 --> 15:00.080
Let's talk about a relay.

15:00.080 --> 15:03.080
Why are relays so common?

15:03.080 --> 15:13.080
Relays are widely used in electronics because they allow larger loads to be powered by low voltage, low amperage electronics.

15:13.080 --> 15:28.080
A relay is an electromechanical component consisting of a coil within which a magnetic field is created such that it closes or opens a contact.

15:28.080 --> 15:35.080
The symbol of the relay is what you see drawn above the component in the figure on the right side of the slide.

15:35.080 --> 15:44.080
Looking at this symbol, we see that the left side is the electrical part or control circuit,

15:44.080 --> 15:49.080
coil in which the magnetic field is created by application of a voltage,

15:49.080 --> 15:58.080
while the right side is the mechanical part or power circuit, which goes to mechanically close the contacts.

15:58.080 --> 16:03.080
The contacts are called normally closed or normally open.

16:03.080 --> 16:10.080
When the relay is not energized, it has one contact already closed, which is called normally closed.

16:10.080 --> 16:19.080
When a voltage is applied and the relay is energized, it goes to close another contact opening the normally closed.

16:19.080 --> 16:23.080
This other contact is called normally open.

16:23.080 --> 16:32.080
The contact you see below in the figure of the symbol of the relay indicated by the number 30 is the common contact.

16:32.080 --> 16:39.080
Now we introduce two concepts.

16:39.080 --> 16:47.080
HSD high side driver switch and LSD low side driver switch.

16:47.080 --> 16:57.080
An HSD high side driver switch relay is defined as a relay that in order to be commanded active,

16:57.080 --> 17:04.080
the control unit that manages its control needs to use a high potential.

17:04.080 --> 17:07.080
In fact, the channel is called active high.

17:07.080 --> 17:13.080
As a result, the other pin of the control coil will be connected to a low reference potential.

17:13.080 --> 17:14.080
A ground to be clear.

17:14.080 --> 17:26.080
The LSD low side driver switch relay is defined as a relay which in order to be commanded active,

17:26.080 --> 17:32.080
the control unit that manages its control needs to use a low potential.

17:32.080 --> 17:39.080
It follows that the control coil will have one of the two pins connected to a high reference potential,

17:39.080 --> 17:45.080
which may be, for example, battery voltage.

17:45.080 --> 18:00.080
In the evolution of electronic systems in the automotive area, control units, ECUs, have been controlling and managing various actuators by using PWM commands.

18:00.080 --> 18:08.080
For example, diesel pressure regulator, EGR, VGT, solenoid valve actuator and so on.

18:08.080 --> 18:13.080
PWM means pulse width modulation.

18:13.080 --> 18:25.080
PWM control is a square wave signal that changes by varying the width of the positive pulse duty cycle in the same unit of time, frequency.

18:25.080 --> 18:34.080
A PWM signal is characterized by fixed frequency and variable duty cycle.

18:34.080 --> 18:48.080
Duty cycle, by definition, is the ratio of the time when the square wave takes a high value to the period T, where the period T is the inverse of the frequency.

18:48.080 --> 19:05.080
It follows that, for example, if we have a duty cycle of 20%, it corresponds to a square wave that takes on a high value 20% of the time and takes a low value in the remaining 80%.

19:05.080 --> 19:32.080
An electronic control unit provides some specific actuators that need PWM control with a fixed frequency square wave signal that is variable cycle modulated.

19:32.080 --> 19:36.080
Here, then, this is the PWM.

19:36.080 --> 19:51.080
For example, the ECU command will therefore be a signal that will feed the solenoid valve coil and will be able to open it completely, close it completely, and most important, partialize its opening.

19:51.080 --> 20:14.080
In practice, the ECU causes the control voltage to varying at such a frequency that the actuator does not move following the instantaneous value of the voltage, but reaches a position that will depend on the proportion between the time the voltage is present and the time the voltage is absent, precisely due to the cycle variation.

20:14.080 --> 20:24.080
An example of a PWM command is the one in the present slide.

20:24.080 --> 20:35.080
Here we can see a niche bridge, which is a rather simple circuit consisting of four electronic switches that drive a load, place it in the center.

20:35.080 --> 20:42.080
They are usually used, for instance, to drive DC motors.

20:42.080 --> 20:57.080
When the four switches S1, S2, S3, S4 are all open, the load, let's say the case of a DC electric motor, for example, that is indicated by letter M, is not energized.

20:57.080 --> 21:08.080
The four switches never close all at once, but in pairs, specifically S1 with S4 and S2 with S3.

21:08.080 --> 21:16.080
In this way, they create two closed paths that allow current to flow within the circuit.

21:16.080 --> 21:26.080
In fact, when, for example, S1 and S4 are closed, the motor that is energized turns in one direction.

21:26.080 --> 21:41.080
When the other two switches, S2 and S3, close instead, the motor is powered and turns in the opposite direction since we have practically reversed the polarity of the voltage applied to the motor.

21:41.080 --> 21:50.080
It is clear that, in theory, the four switches can be opened and closed independently of the others.

21:50.080 --> 22:02.080
But in practice, we avoid these configurations different from these two because, for instance, if they are all closed at the same time, a short circuit would result.

22:02.080 --> 22:08.080
The H-bridge allows the motor to be controlled only in the on-off mode.

22:08.080 --> 22:19.080
In the on state, the motor is energized and turns, one way or the other, at the maximum speed allowed by the V-in voltage.

22:19.080 --> 22:23.080
While in the off state, the motor is not powered.

22:23.080 --> 22:32.080
However, in most practical applications, continuous regulation of the rotational speed is required.

22:32.080 --> 22:42.080
And this can be achieved by controlling the four switches using pulse width modulation, so using PWM control.

22:42.080 --> 22:53.080
And this will then cause that during the on time, the motor is energized and gradually increases its speed.

22:53.080 --> 23:03.080
While during the off time, the power supply is zero and the motor, if it was turning before, now tends to break.

23:03.080 --> 23:04.080
Thank you.

23:04.080 --> 23:19.080
Thank you very much.

23:19.080 --> 23:24.080
Thank you.

